# Every classification the queries make, against the classes it declares, over every loaded
# document. One row per (relation, class): the relation that classifies, the type its classes
# belong to, what one counted row is, and how many landed in the class.
#
# The classes come from their type
#   The laws in algebra/ check that each row lands in exactly one class, whatever the classes are
#   called. Which classes there are is the type's to say: each set here is an enum, so a misspelt
#   class fails when the query is parsed. Neither shows which classes are empty, and an empty class
#   is often what a reader is looking for, so this relation lists every class, empty or not.
#
# The corpus and the fixtures, counted together
#   It counts the corpus and the fixtures together, because it asks whether anything in this
#   repository produces the class at all. A class no document reaches under any scope is a class no
#   relation here can produce, and folds/class_subjects.sqlc holds epistemics/class_domain.sqlc to
#   exactly that.
#   It is not a reading of the evidence. What a chosen set of documents shows is a different
#   question, and reports/class_census.sqlc answers it. `assets/fixtures/README.md` says why a class
#   nothing reaches means opposite things in the two places.
#
# `subject` is what one counted row is
#   The relations count different things, so `balls` compares down one relation's own rows and
#   nowhere else. epistemics/class_sets.sqlc declares what each relation counts, and this carries it
#   beside the count, so a reader never has to guess. The join is LEFT, so a relation missing from
#   that roster arrives with a NULL `subject` and algebra/class_domain.sqlc reports it, instead of
#   its classes vanishing.
#
# `filed_balls` holds the `filed` flag
#   epistemics/class_sets.sqlc declares whether a relation classifies rows a document filed, and
#   this counts the rows that actually carry a filing. algebra/class_domain.sqlc compares the two,
#   so a relation whose rows stop carrying a filing, or start to, fails instead of quietly narrowing
#   what it reports. It is one more column of the same count, and costs no extra pass.
#
# `ord` keeps the declared order
#   Each type's classes come out in the order the type declares them, which is the order of the
#   `CASE` that assigns them. Grouped alone, a reader could sort only by the class's name, which is
#   alphabetical and says nothing, and the order carries a reading of its own:
#   layers/exposure_scope.sqlc explains its order in prose.
#
# `codomain` comes from `pg_typeof`
#   Taking the type from the class itself lets a caller hold every enum in schema `public` against
#   this relation through the catalog, which Postgres writes. A class set added to schema.ddl with
#   no arm in epistemics/class_cells.sqlc fails `examples/combinatorics`.
-- epistemics/class_cells.sqlc counted per class over every loaded document, with its declared unit.
SELECT k.relation, k.codomain, k.class, s.subject,
       count(k.ball)   AS balls,
       count(k.filing) AS filed_balls,
       k.ord
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/class_cells.sqlc)
) k
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/class_sets.sqlc)
) s ON s.relation = k.relation
GROUP BY k.relation, k.codomain, k.class, s.subject, k.ord
